WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:01.173
(whooshing)

00:00:01.173 --> 00:00:02.872
- [Man] Lift off, lift off.

00:00:02.872 --> 00:00:05.955
(instrumental music)

00:00:23.239 --> 00:00:25.322
- [Narrator] Meet Bjarke.

00:00:40.452 --> 00:00:42.858
Basically, he's a tinkerer,
and he and his colleagues

00:00:42.858 --> 00:00:44.969
have cobbled together
this simple let of sensors

00:00:44.969 --> 00:00:46.867
that will help these kids.

00:00:46.867 --> 00:00:48.006
- I'm Angelique Swartz.

00:00:48.006 --> 00:00:48.839
- Tara Sadat.

00:00:48.839 --> 00:00:50.374
- Rabelani Nenungwi.

00:00:50.374 --> 00:00:52.458
- [Narrator] Get a lot closer to space.

00:00:52.458 --> 00:00:53.836
That's possible because well,

00:00:53.836 --> 00:00:55.503
Bjarke, you tell ‘em.

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:08.925
Okay, so here's how it works.

00:01:08.925 --> 00:01:10.717
The students get their hands on Bjarke's

00:01:10.717 --> 00:01:12.695
XinaBox sensors, those ones,

00:01:12.695 --> 00:01:15.040
which give real time
readings on things like

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:18.057
temperature, humidity,
and UV light levels.

00:01:18.057 --> 00:01:19.956
Then, they're built
into Bjarke's satellite,

00:01:19.956 --> 00:01:22.325
which is loaded on to a rocket, like this,

00:01:22.325 --> 00:01:25.536
and then, they're sent
into lower Earth orbit.

00:01:25.536 --> 00:01:27.369
- [Man] We have stage one separation.

00:01:27.369 --> 00:01:28.243
- [Narrator] Cool, right?

00:01:28.243 --> 00:01:29.121
- Pretty cool.

00:01:29.121 --> 00:01:32.224
These little things are
just gonna be in space

00:01:32.224 --> 00:01:35.297
recording everything we don't know about.

00:01:35.297 --> 00:01:37.777
What is it like up there
where we've never been before

00:01:37.777 --> 00:01:41.435
and it's really cool because
we will get to see that.

00:01:41.435 --> 00:01:42.991
- [Narrator] So, just like that,

00:01:42.991 --> 00:01:45.126
a bunch of kids in South
Africa will be operating

00:01:45.126 --> 00:01:47.051
their own classroom ground control,

00:01:47.051 --> 00:01:49.856
playing with data taken
straight from outer space.

00:01:49.856 --> 00:01:51.368
(instrumental music)

00:01:51.368 --> 00:01:54.451
And that's all part of Bjarke's plan.

00:02:13.624 --> 00:02:14.622
Huh, that's clever.

00:02:14.622 --> 00:02:16.481
Wonder if it's working.

00:02:16.481 --> 00:02:17.643
- Thinking of doing medicine.

00:02:17.643 --> 00:02:21.557
- Plan on studying for
about, uh, seven to 12 years

00:02:21.557 --> 00:02:23.769
to become a neurologist.

00:02:23.769 --> 00:02:26.975
- One day I would actually
want to work with these things

00:02:26.975 --> 00:02:29.476
because they are just so interesting.

00:02:29.476 --> 00:02:32.905
Technology and math and
science and engineering

00:02:32.905 --> 00:02:35.300
has made all of what we are now possible,

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:37.660
and that's why it's important.

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:40.827
(instrumental music)

